### READING (BlackThrush, frankenscipy-921i0)

## 1. THE LEVER (self A/B, both arms one binary) — MAINTENANCE, not a win

    n=20,000     resort 0.684ms  pass 0.444ms   1.5387x   share 35.01%   DECIDED
    n=200,000    resort 9.332ms  pass 6.436ms   1.4706x   share 32.00%   DECIDED
    n=2,000,000  resort 124.109ms pass 80.330ms 1.5403x   share 35.08%   DECIDED

All three DECIDED at the replicate level: the candidate's p10 clears the null's
p90 with room at every size (e.g. n=2M candidate [1.5273,1.5622] against a null
[0.9779,1.0182]). A/A nulls 1.0017 / 1.0018 / 0.9934 — tight, on a pinned core at
loadavg ~11.

This is ours-against-ours and is MAINTENANCE by the campaign's own rule. It is not
the win.

BYTE-IDENTITY HELD ON THE REAL FIXTURE: bitmism=0 at every size, checked before any
timing was believed. The MUST-HIT control is satisfied rather than assumed —
tie_groups 5,000 / 50,000 / 500,000 and tie_mass 3.75e-8 / 3.75e-10 / 3.75e-12,
recomputed over the VALUES by exact equality, so no row here describes a tie-free
fixture on which the lever's equivalence argument would be vacuous.
branch=asymptotic at every size, so every row exercised the path the tie correction
actually lives on.

## 2. GATE (a): the share is LARGE, and its predicted TREND did not appear

The bead predicted the share would GROW with n, on the model "an O(n log n) sort
removed from an O(n) remainder". It does not: 35.01% -> 32.00% -> 35.08%, FLAT.
Reported that way round because the prediction failed, not because the number is
convenient. The bead's stop condition was "if the sort is a SMALL share at the
sizes callers actually use, report that and STOP" — 32-35% is not small, so the
lever stands, but the mechanism story is weaker than the bead assumed. Kruskal
behaved identically (25-32%, also flat), which is consistent: above n=2^14 the
ranking pass uses a radix argsort, so the removed comparison sort is being weighed
against O(n) work at every size measured, and no growth should be expected.

## 3. THE WIN: live scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu, same invocation

    n=20,000     fsci 0.451ms   scipy 1.183ms    RATIO 2.624x   scipy A/A null 0.9770
    n=200,000    fsci 6.271ms   scipy 14.471ms   RATIO 2.308x   scipy A/A null 0.9933
    n=2,000,000  fsci 81.247ms  scipy 132.946ms  RATIO 1.636x   scipy A/A null 0.9689

FrankenSciPy is 1.64x to 2.62x FASTER than the live incumbent, with the scipy arm
bracketing the fsci arm in the same window so drift lands in the incumbent null
rather than the ratio. The effect is one to two orders of magnitude larger than any
null observed (worst null deviation 3.1%).

PARITY BEFORE ANY RATIO IS BELIEVED: agree=true at every size, U and p both at
relgap 0.000e0 — bit-exact against SciPy, not merely close. Both arms pinned to
method="asymptotic", because SciPy's default "auto" would take the exact null on
small tie-free inputs and time a different algorithm entirely.

## 4. WHAT THE LEVER IS WORTH AGAINST THE INCUMBENT — an ESTIMATE, not a row

The two harnesses agree on the post-lever fsci number at n=2M (80.330ms in the A/B,
81.247ms in the head-to-head, within 1.1%), so composing them is defensible:

    scipy / pre-lever fsci  ~=  132.946 / 124.109  ~=  1.07x

i.e. at n=2,000,000 the redundant sort was very nearly the whole margin — without
it mannwhitneyu sits at roughly parity with SciPy, and with it we are at 1.64x.
This is a COMPOSITION ACROSS TWO BINARIES AND TWO WINDOWS, not a measured row, and
must not be quoted as one. Measuring it directly needs the ORIG arm and the scipy
arm in a single invocation, which neither binary does today.

## 5. WHAT IS NOT MEASURED

The tie-sum accumulation added to the ranking pass runs in BOTH arms of the A/B, so
1.47-1.54x is a LOWER BOUND on the true pre-lever/post-lever ratio, and it says
nothing about whether that accumulation slowed `rankdata`'s other callers.
`mannwhitneyu_alternative` carries the identical edit at lib.rs:31067 and is pinned
byte-identical by the same unit test, but was NOT timed — do not quote these rows
for it.

